      "strengths_deep_dive": [
        "IMANA's operational strength lies in its institutional maturity and governance. With a <cite id=\"11\">board of 12 members</cite>, the organization ensures diverse oversight for its global missions. Its fundraising efficiency is notable, spending only <cite id=\"12\">$0.13 to raise every $1</cite> in FY2024. This efficiency allows more resources to be directed toward its <cite id=\"13\">direct service delivery model</cite>, which includes establishing health clinics and hospitals in refugee-dense regions. Furthermore, the organization has achieved the <cite id=\"14\">Candid Platinum Seal of Transparency</cite>, the highest level of recognition for nonprofit data disclosure."
      ],
      "summary": "The Islamic Medical Association of North America (IMANA) is a long-standing institution that provides medical relief and health programs to underserved communities globally. Founded in <cite id=\"1\">1967</cite>, the organization has spent over five decades bridging the gap between healthcare professionals and humanitarian needs. In FY2024, IMANA managed <cite id=\"2\">$3,675,972 in total revenue</cite>, reflecting a significant <cite id=\"3\">3-year revenue growth rate of 43.4%</cite>. This growth supports a wide range of initiatives, including <cite id=\"4\">foreign medical program support totaling $1,268,016</cite> and domestic medical grants. The organization maintains a <cite id=\"5\">96/100 overall score from Charity Navigator</cite>, which signals a high level of accountability and transparency to its donors. IMANA is particularly effective at <cite id=\"6\">leveraging medical volunteers</cite> to provide direct patient care in crisis zones such as Gaza, Sudan, and Syria. While its financial health is stable, its <cite id=\"7\">program expense ratio of 68.7%</cite> is moderate compared to some peers, though it remains within acceptable industry standards for organizations managing complex international logistics.",
